Problem Statement
Diffusion language models generate text by iteratively denoising a corrupted sequence, which means the model performs different kinds of computation at different denoising timesteps. Early timesteps tend to operate on highly corrupted inputs and are expected to recover coarse semantic and structural information, while later timesteps operate on cleaner inputs and are expected to refine lexical choices and local details. Recent work on diffusion models and diffusion-MoE architectures suggests that routing behavior can vary meaningfully across denoising stages, and that timestep-aware routing may improve both specialization and efficiency. (arXiv)
In standard MoE routing, expert selection is often conditioned only on token features or hidden states, without explicit awareness of the denoising timestep. In a diffusion language model, however, this may be suboptimal because the same token position can require qualitatively different processing at different stages of denoising. I therefore hypothesize that conditioning the routing gate on the denoising timestep will encourage stage-specific expert specialization, allowing some experts to focus on early coarse reconstruction and others to focus on later fine-grained refinement. (arXiv)
The central research question is: Does timestep-aware routing improve expert specialization and generative performance in diffusion language models compared with a static MoE router? I will investigate this by augmenting the router with a learned timestep embedding and comparing the resulting model against an otherwise identical baseline MoE-DLM whose router does not receive timestep information. Expert specialization will be analyzed through expert-activation patterns across denoising timesteps, while model quality will be evaluated using perplexity and bits per dimension (BPD), both of which are used in discrete diffusion language modeling studies. (arXiv)

Methodology
I will implement two models based on the same diffusion language model backbone and the same sparse MoE architecture. The baseline model will use a static router that selects experts from token hidden states alone. The proposed model will use a timestep-aware router that receives the same routing inputs plus a learned embedding of the denoising timestep. All other factors — including number of experts, top-k routing, parameter budget, optimizer, training schedule, and dataset — will be held constant so that any performance difference can be attributed specifically to timestep conditioning in the router. This controlled comparison is important because recent diffusion-MoE work shows that routing design itself can materially affect quality and efficiency. (arXiv)
Formally, for token position (i) at denoising timestep (t), the baseline router computes expert logits as a function of the token representation (h_{i,t}), while the proposed router computes expert logits as a function of both (h_{i,t}) and a timestep embedding (e_t). In practice, the timestep embedding can be produced from a sinusoidal or learned timestep representation followed by a small MLP, and concatenated with the hidden state before the routing projection. This design is consistent with the broader diffusion modeling practice of conditioning internal computations on timestep information. (arXiv)
Model performance will be measured using perplexity and bits per dimension (BPD) on held-out validation and test sets. Perplexity remains a widely used measure of language modeling quality, while BPD is commonly reported in diffusion modeling as a normalized likelihood-based metric. Because evaluation conventions in discrete diffusion language modeling are still evolving, reporting both metrics will make the comparison more robust and easier to interpret. (arXiv)
To quantify expert specialization, I will log router assignments during inference or validation and aggregate expert usage statistics across denoising timesteps. These will be visualized as expert activation heatmaps with experts on one axis and timestep buckets on the other. If timestep-aware routing induces stage-specific specialization, I expect the heatmaps to show more structured and non-uniform activation patterns than the baseline, where expert usage may be flatter or less interpretable across timesteps. To support the qualitative heatmap analysis, I will also compute quantitative specialization measures such as timestep-conditioned expert entropy, variance of expert usage across timestep buckets, or mutual information between expert identity and timestep bucket. (arXiv)
To ensure that the results are not confounded by routing collapse or load imbalance, I will monitor auxiliary routing statistics during training, including per-expert token counts, router entropy, and load-balancing losses. This is important because diffusion-MoE work highlights expert imbalance and routing instability as practical concerns in sparse diffusion architectures. (arXiv)

Expected Outcomes
I expect the timestep-aware router to produce clearer expert specialization by denoising stage than the static router. In particular, I expect some experts to be activated more frequently at early timesteps, where the model must recover broad semantic and structural information from highly corrupted inputs, and other experts to become more active at later timesteps, where the task shifts toward lexical refinement and local consistency. This expectation is consistent with the broader observation that different denoising timesteps play different functional roles in diffusion generation. (arXiv)
I also expect timestep-aware routing to improve overall model quality, reflected in lower perplexity and improved BPD relative to the baseline. The reason is that timestep information gives the router an additional signal about the type of computation required at each denoising stage, enabling more appropriate expert allocation than a static routing rule. If this hypothesis holds, the proposed model should not only perform better but also yield more interpretable routing behavior. (arXiv)
More broadly, a positive result would support the idea that MoE routing in diffusion language models should be designed as a stage-aware computation policy, rather than simply reusing static routing strategies inherited from autoregressive MoE language models. Such a finding would provide a useful architectural principle for future sparse diffusion language models and could motivate richer routing mechanisms conditioned jointly on timestep, token identity, uncertainty, or denoising state. (arXiv)
